---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import NuCaPainter from '../../components/NuCaPainter.astro';
import CnnRow from '../../components/CnnRow.astro';
import Selector from '../../components/Selector.astro';
import Training from '../../components/Training.astro';
import Bench from '../../components/Bench.astro';
import Tex from '../../components/Tex.astro';
import data from '../../data/nuca-cnn.json';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
// The paper this page is built on lives in the publications collection, so its links stay in one place.
const pubs = (await getCollection('publications')).map((p) => p.data);
const paper = pubs.find((p) => p.id === 'cnn-nonuniform');
const classification = pubs.find((p) => p.id === 'cnn-classification');
const doi = `https://doi.org/${paper?.doi ?? '10.1007/978-3-031-71552-5_11'}`;
const arxiv = paper?.url ?? 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.02722';
const classDoi = `https://doi.org/${classification?.doi ?? '10.1007/978-3-031-81097-8_3'}`;
const repo = 'https://github.com/mrollier/emulating-and-learning-CAs';
const tree = `${repo}/tree/${data.source.commit}`;
// One tab per step of the argument; the hash picks the tab, so a step can be linked.
const tabs = [
  { id: 'question', name: 'The question' },
  { id: 'forty', name: 'Forty weights' },
  { id: 'layer', name: 'One more layer' },
  { id: 'learning', name: 'Learning the weights' },
  { id: 'faster', name: 'Faster?' },
  { id: 'verdict', name: 'Verdict' },
];
---
<Base title="The automaton is the network" description="A non-uniform cellular automaton written as a convolutional neural network with its weights set by hand, exact to the last bit: the 40 weights, the extra layer that picks each cell's rule, training the weights live, and the benchmarks of my ACRI 2024 paper." wide>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>The automaton is the network</h1>
    <p>
      A cellular automaton updates every cell from a small window around it. A convolutional neural network, the kind
      behind most image recognition, computes every pixel of its next layer from a small window around it. The two are
      close relatives, and the family resemblance is useful both ways: neural networks need lots of examples of
      cellular automata to learn to classify them, and cellular automata could use the heavily optimised software that
      neural networks run on. For the elementary cellular automaton the match was known to be exact. My question was the
      non-uniform one, where every cell may follow its own rule, which is exactly what breaks the defining habit of a
      convolution: the same weights everywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      The answer is in a <a href={doi}>conference paper</a> with Aisling Daly, Odemir Bruno and Jan Baetens, for the
      cellular-automata conference ACRI 2024. In one sentence:
    </p>
    <p class="claim">
      A non-uniform cellular automaton fits exactly in a convolutional neural network, with one extra layer that picks
      each cell's rule; a deep-learning framework then simulates many large non-uniform automata faster than a
      dedicated cellular-automaton package.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first half is a construction, and the page runs it: the network's weights are written down by hand, nothing is
      learned, and the result agrees with a direct simulation bit for bit, down to the paper's own figures. The second
      half is a benchmark, and there the story continued after the paper: I came back to it two years later with better
      ways of timing, and the <a href="#faster">Faster?</a> tab tells both parts. The tabs follow the paper: why bother,
      the 40 weights of the elementary case, the one layer the non-uniform case adds, training those weights instead of
      writing them down, and the speed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code is public, in <a href={repo}>mrollier/emulating-and-learning-CAs</a>, with a test for every claim of the
      paper; the published figure inputs and timings on this page come from it. Everything else is computed in your
      browser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabbed">
    <nav class="tabs" aria-label="Steps">
      <ul role="tablist" aria-orientation="vertical">
        {tabs.map((t, i) => <li><a role="tab" id={`tab-${t.id}`} href={`#${t.id}`} aria-controls={t.id} aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} tabindex={i === 0 ? 0 : -1}>{t.name}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="panels">

      <section role="tabpanel" id="question" aria-labelledby="tab-question" tabindex="0">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>The question</h2>
        <p>
          An elementary cellular automaton is a row of cells, each 0 or 1, that all update at once, each from itself and
          its two neighbours, all by the same rule. Relax any of that and you land in one of the families of <a href="/demos/taxonomy/">the taxonomy</a>. Let different cells follow different rules and you have a
          non-uniform cellular automaton, a νCA. In general the allocation of rules to cells may change over time too;
          the paper, like most of the literature, keeps it fixed in time and lets it vary in space, on a finite ring of
          cells. Pick the rules and paint the allocation:
        </p>
      </div>
      <NuCaPainter />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The freedom is enormous. A ring of <Tex tex="N" /> cells with one elementary rule has <Tex tex="2^N" /> starting
          rows for each of the 256 rules; with <Tex tex="N_R" /> rules to allocate, there are
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="2^N \times N_R^{\,N}" />
        <p>
          combinations of a start and an allocation. So much variety leaves little room for general theorems, except in
          special cases far from any application, and the way forward is empirical: simulate many of them and sort what
          they do. Sorting spacetime diagrams is, at heart, recognising images, which is what convolutional networks are
          good at, and training such a network takes a great many examples. Both ends of that pipeline call for fast,
          massive simulation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here the locality of a convolution helps twice. A network can be trained to recognise cellular automata, and it
          can also <em>be</em> one: Gilpin showed in 2019 that the update of any cellular automaton fits in the
          architecture of a convolutional network, either by choosing the weights cleverly or by training them. The
          next tab does the choosing for the elementary case; the one after adds what the non-uniform case needs.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="forty" aria-labelledby="tab-forty" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Forty weights</h2>
        <p>
          One update of an elementary cellular automaton takes two layers. The first reads each cell's neighbourhood and
          says which of the eight possible patterns it is. The paper describes it in two steps: a convolution of width 3
          with the fixed weights (4, 2, 1), which turns the three cells into a number from 0 to 7, followed by a one-hot
          encoding, eight channels of which exactly one is 1. The code does both at once, with eight detector channels
          and a ReLU, <Tex tex="\max(0, \cdot)" />. Channel <Tex tex="i" /> looks for the pattern whose bits
          spell <Tex tex="i" />, with weight 1 on the cells that should be 1, weight <Tex tex="-\omega" /> on the cells
          that should be 0, and as bias 1 minus the number of 1s in the pattern, <Tex tex="h_i" />:
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="d_i(x) = \max\Bigl(0,\ \sum_{j=-1}^{1} w_{ij}\, s_{x+j} + 1 - h_i\Bigr), \qquad w_{ij} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if bit } j \text{ of } i \text{ is } 1, \\ -\omega & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}" />
        <p>
          On its own pattern the sum is exactly 1; on any other it is at most 0, as long as <Tex tex="\omega \ge 1" />.
          The second layer is a convolution of width 1 whose eight weights are the rule table <Tex tex="\phi(i)" />, so
          that the next state is
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="s'_x = \sum_{i=0}^{7} \phi(i)\, d_i(x)." />
        <p>
          That is 3 weights and a bias for each of the eight detectors, plus the eight entries of the rule table: 40
          numbers, none of them learned, and the cost grows only with the number of cells. The figure spells the
          arithmetic out on the example of the paper's Fig. 2, rule 54 on 32 cells; show the sums before the ReLU and drag <Tex tex="\omega" /> to see that the answer does not move.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CnnRow rule={54} n={32} start={data.fig3.x} />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Feeding the output back in gives the next tick, and so on: the network performs one global update per pass, and
          a spacetime diagram of <Tex tex="T" /> rows takes <Tex tex="T-1" /> passes. There is no shortcut through time.
          Every update needs the one before it, a form of the computational irreducibility of cellular automata, so the
          ticks cannot be farmed out in parallel; only the cells and the samples can.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="layer" aria-labelledby="tab-layer" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>One more layer</h2>
        <p>
          In a convolution every cell is treated with the same weights. In a νCA they are not, so something has to give.
          The paper keeps the two layers above and adds a third. The rule-table layer gets one output channel per rule:
          the whole next row as if every cell followed rule 1, the whole next row as if every cell followed rule 2, and so
          on. Then a selection layer keeps, for each cell, the channel of the rule it was allocated. That is wasteful,
          computing every rule everywhere, but it keeps the network easy to read, and it leaves room to train more
          weights than strictly needed, which is how deep learning usually works.
        </p>
        <p>
          The selection can be written in two ways. A locally connected layer is a convolution whose weights are not
          shared, one weight per rule per cell. A dense layer flattens the <Tex tex="N_R" /> candidate rows into one
          vector of <Tex tex="N_R N" /> numbers and connects every one of them to every output cell: a matrix of <Tex tex="N_R N \times N" /> weights set by hand to 0 or 1, almost all of them 0. The locally connected layer is
          the dense one with the useless connections cut. Leaving out the zero biases, the two networks have
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="(3+1) \times 8 + 8 N_R + N_R N \qquad \text{and} \qquad (3+1) \times 8 + 8 N_R + N_R N^2" />
        <p>
          parameters, 352 and 8288 for the eight rules and 32 cells of the paper's Fig. 4. The figure takes one update
          apart: the input, the candidate row of every rule with the cells that keep it highlighted, the allocation, the
          output. Beside it, the run over all ticks; below, the selection weights. Fig. 1 of the paper, rules 30 and 90,
          comes first; Fig. 4 adds an allocation that moves one cell per tick, which only means swapping the selection
          weights between updates. Both diagrams come out cell for cell as published.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Selector />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          For comparison the paper takes CellPyLib, the usual Python package for cellular automata. A νCA is easy to set
          up there: an array says which rule applies where and when, and its <code>evolve</code> method does the rest. But
          a non-uniform rule defeats the cache that CellPyLib keeps of neighbourhoods it has already seen
          (<code>memoize</code>), the shortcut that normally makes it fast. With an allocation that does not change in
          time, the network's weights never change either: it is built once and fed any number of starting rows.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="learning" aria-labelledby="tab-learning" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Learning the weights</h2>
        <p>
          The weights can be computed, but for cellular automata without a known rule they would have to be learned, and
          the paper shows in its Fig. 3 that the same architecture can be trained: start from random weights, show it
          rows and their images under rule 54, and let gradient descent shrink the error until the network gets every
          cell right. The figure below does that in your browser. The network has 41 numbers (the trained rule-table
          layer gets a bias), and since it sees only three cells at a time, the eight neighbourhoods are all it can learn
          from, so they are its training batch. On the left, as in the paper's figure, the error on each cell of the
          published example after every epoch of 64 steps.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Training />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The figure opens with the paper's recipe, minus one trick described below, and that recipe does not always
          work. In the training study I added to the
          repository this year, after the paper, it reached an exact network in 88% of runs over all rules and seeds,
          and only thanks to a filter that throws away unpromising starts; without the filter, 47%. Rule 1 never works.
          The culprit is the output: a ReLU on the rule-table layer, before the tanh. A neighbourhood that should give 1
          but starts below 0 gets no gradient through the ReLU and stays stuck there, and the figure marks such
          neighbourhoods in red. Rule 1 is the extreme case: its only 1 comes from 000, where inputs of 0 and biases of 0
          leave every channel at exactly 0 from the start. Drop that ReLU, read the output as a logit with cross-entropy
          as the loss, feed the states as ±1 and use softplus detectors, and the same 41 parameters train to an exact
          network in every one of 262,144 runs, every rule with 1024 seeds.
        </p>
        <p>
          The button under the figure repeats the experiment here, every rule with eight seeds and the eight
          neighbourhoods as the batch. It finds about 47 to 48% for the paper's head without the filter (a run or two can
          land differently between browsers), close to the study's 46.7%, and all 2048 runs exact for the reliable recipe. One more finding: trained networks are exact, but none of the
          4.5 million exact ones in the study had found the one-hot detectors of the previous tab. Gradient descent finds
          its own, distributed code, as the weights under the figure show.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="faster" aria-labelledby="tab-faster" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Faster?</h2>
        <p>
          Computing every rule for every cell and then throwing most of it away should be slow, and a program written for
          νCAs alone would certainly beat it. The bet of the paper is a different one: TensorFlow, like PyTorch, is
          developed at such a scale that its out-of-the-box speed might win anyway, with less effort. The paper times
          CellPyLib and the two networks in four scenarios, varying one quantity at a time:
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">scenario</th><th scope="col">rules</th><th scope="col">ticks</th><th scope="col">cells</th><th scope="col">νCAs</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">more rules</th><td>1 to 256</td><td>32</td><td>256</td><td>32</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">more ticks</th><td>4</td><td>10 to 100</td><td>64</td><td>32</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">more cells</th><td>4</td><td>32</td><td>32 to 256</td><td>32</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">more νCAs</th><td>4</td><td>32</td><td>32</td><td>1 to 1024</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Each νCA starts from its own random row with the same allocation, so each network is built once, and every time
          is the mean of ten runs on one laptop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Bench />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The paper reads four things off these curves. The number of rules hardly matters, except for the dense network
          at the high end, and for few rules the dense network is clearly the fastest. All three methods grow linearly
          with the number of ticks. CellPyLib's time is proportional to the number of cells, the locally connected
          network's too but after a higher fixed cost, and the dense network's barely grows at all. And with many νCAs
          the networks pay off: from about a hundred of them on they are the fastest, and at 1024 they take 8 seconds
          where CellPyLib takes 39. Its conclusion is that TensorFlow is
          the better choice for many νCAs of many cells, which is exactly what an empirical study or the training of a
          classifier needs, and, more surprisingly, that the dense network beats the locally connected one almost
          everywhere, although the latter is the former with most connections cut. The framework is so well optimised
          for big matrix products that the bigger model wins.
        </p>
        <h3>Two years later</h3>
        <p>
          In September 2026 I re-ran the benchmark on the same laptop, and the dashed lines show it: the crossovers and
          the order of the three methods hold. Then I timed it differently, and the picture changed more. The networks
          had been run with one <code>model.predict</code> call per tick, and each such call costs about 40 ms whatever
          the size of the network: the fixed cost of a few seconds in the curves is that overhead, not the network.
          Run without that overhead, compiled into a single function, the fastest of the networks is 13 to 2000 times
          faster than in the paper and beats CellPyLib by a factor of 30 to 480; even the simplest fix beats CellPyLib at
          every point. But plain vectorised numpy, a table lookup per cell per tick, is faster still, in 97 of the 102
          settings measured: the networks win only for a handful of small diagrams, and then need several hundred
          diagrams to earn back their compilation. The full
          report is in the <a href={`${tree}/experiments/benchmarks/REPORT.md`}>repository</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the paper's comparison stands, and with more room than it claimed. What it measured, though, is the ease of a
          framework against a package that cannot cache, not the fastest way to simulate a νCA. The paper says as much in
          its last lines, which do not claim TensorFlow is the computationally optimal solution, only an educational,
          ergonomic and flexible one.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="verdict" aria-labelledby="tab-verdict" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>What it adds up to</h2>
        <p>
          The paper's main conclusion:
        </p>
        <p class="claim">
          A non-uniform cellular automaton fits exactly in a convolutional neural network, with one extra layer that picks
          each cell's rule; a deep-learning framework then simulates many large non-uniform automata faster than a
          dedicated cellular-automaton package.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first half is exact, and stays exact: 40 hand-set weights for the elementary case, a selection layer for the
          non-uniform one, and agreement with a direct simulation in every cell, here and in the repository's tests. Non-uniformity
          of the rule is, to the framework, nothing more than weights that are not shared; the automaton is the
          architecture. The second half holds against CellPyLib, even more clearly once the networks are run properly, but
          it is not the last word on speed: a lookup table in numpy is faster. The value of the construction lies
          elsewhere. A cellular automaton written in the vocabulary of a deep-learning framework can be trained, combined
          with learned layers and run on the same hardware, and the same weights that simulate it are the ones a network
          would have to learn to recognise it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both directions continued. On networks instead of rows, the same idea becomes a graph neural network: each node
          averages its neighbours' states and looks the result up in its rule, which ran every simulation of the later
          chapters of <a href="/demos/thesis/#cnn">my thesis</a>. And the other way round, a <a href={classDoi}>companion
          chapter</a> asks what a convolutional network learns when it is not told the rule but must sort automata by
          their behaviour.
        </p>
        <p>
          A caveat about this page. The networks here are written in plain JavaScript, not TensorFlow; they do the same
          arithmetic, and the figures check it against the published diagrams, but none of the speed claims are timed in
          your browser. The timings are the repository's.
        </p>

        <h2>Read more</h2>
        <ul class="pubs">
          <li>
            <a href={doi}>Efficient simulation of non-uniform cellular automata with a convolutional neural network</a>
            <div class="meta"><strong>M. Rollier</strong>, A. J. Daly, O. M. Bruno, J. M. Baetens</div>
            <div class="meta">Cellular Automata (ACRI 2024), Lecture Notes in Computer Science 14978, pp. 121–131, 2024 · <a href={arxiv}>preprint</a></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={repo}>mrollier/emulating-and-learning-CAs</a>
            <div class="meta">The code: the emulators, every figure, a test per claim, and the training and benchmark studies of 2026. This page uses release 1.0.1.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.100.032402">Cellular automata as convolutional neural networks</a>
            <div class="meta">W. Gilpin, Physical Review E 100, 032402, 2019. The general construction, and how such networks learn.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.03608">CellPyLib: a Python library for working with cellular automata</a>
            <div class="meta">L. M. Antunes, Journal of Open Source Software 6, 3608, 2021. The benchmark the paper compares with.</div>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="meta">
          Keys once a figure has focus: the arrows step through the updates, n draws a new νCA or a new seed, r trains
          again. The address bar follows the settings, so a view can be linked.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .tabbed { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 10rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 2rem; align-items: start; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .tabs { position: sticky; top: 1rem; min-width: 0; }
  .tabs ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.15rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .tabs a {
    display: block; padding: 0.3rem 0.8rem; margin-left: -1px; border-left: 2px solid transparent;
    color: var(--muted); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .tabs a:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  .tabs a[aria-selected="true"] { color: var(--ink); border-left-color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }
  .panels { min-width: 0; }
  [role="tabpanel"]:focus { outline: none; }
  [role="tabpanel"] > .measure > h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .claim { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 1rem; font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.35rem 0.8rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  thead th { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: normal; color: var(--muted); }
  tbody th { font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .tabbed { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; }
    .tabs { top: 0; z-index: 5; background: var(--bg); padding: 0.4rem 0; }
    .tabs ul { flex-direction: row; overflow-x: auto; overflow-y: hidden; overscroll-behavior-x: contain; border-left: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); gap: 0; }
    .tabs a { white-space: nowrap; margin: 0 0 -1px; border-left: 0; border-bottom: 2px solid transparent; padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem; }
    .tabs a[aria-selected="true"] { border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // The hash picks the tab: a tab's own id, or the id of anything inside a tab. Arrow keys walk the list.
  const tabs = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[role="tab"]'));
  const panels = tabs.map((t) => document.getElementById(t.getAttribute('aria-controls')!)!);
  const box = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.tabbed')!;

  function show(id: string, scroll: boolean) {
    let i = tabs.findIndex((t) => t.getAttribute('aria-controls') === id), target: HTMLElement | null = null;
    if (i < 0) { target = id ? document.getElementById(id) : null; const p = target?.closest<HTMLElement>('[role="tabpanel"]'); i = p ? panels.indexOf(p) : 0; }
    if (i < 0) i = 0;
    tabs.forEach((t, k) => { t.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(k === i)); t.tabIndex = k === i ? 0 : -1; panels[k].hidden = k !== i; });
    // an anchor inside a tab scrolls to it; a tab change only scrolls back up when the reader was deep in the previous one
    if (target) target.scrollIntoView({ block: 'start' });
    else if (scroll && scrollY > box.offsetTop) box.scrollIntoView({ block: 'start' });
  }
  const fromHash = () => show(decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1)), true);
  addEventListener('hashchange', fromHash);
  fromHash();

  document.querySelector('[role="tablist"]')!.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    const k = (e as KeyboardEvent).key, i = tabs.indexOf(document.activeElement as HTMLAnchorElement);
    if (i < 0) return;
    const n = tabs.length, j = k === 'ArrowDown' || k === 'ArrowRight' ? (i + 1) % n : k === 'ArrowUp' || k === 'ArrowLeft' ? (i + n - 1) % n : k === 'Home' ? 0 : k === 'End' ? n - 1 : -1;
    if (j < 0) return;
    e.preventDefault(); location.hash = tabs[j].getAttribute('aria-controls')!; tabs[j].focus();
  });
</script>
